        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix: release stream reader lock, bound error body reads, cancel cloned body",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\r\n\r\nFixes three issues where HTTP response bodies are not properly cleaned up or bounded in failure paths:\r\n\r\n1. **Ollama stream reader lock never released**: `response.body.getReader()` lock was never released, relying solely on implicit fetch cleanup which can delay socket reuse in some Node.js versions.\r\n2. **HTTP error body read without byte limit**: The `readResponseBodySnippet` fallback used `response.arrayBuffer()` (and previously `response.text()`) on the body-less path, materializing the entire error body before slicing the buffer. A reverse proxy returning a multi??undred??B body could OOM the gateway before `limits.maxBytes` was applied.\r\n3. **Firecrawl error-path body not explicitly cancelled**: After cloning and consuming a response body for JSON error parsing, the original body was not explicitly cancelled.\r\n\r\n## Why This Change Was Made\r\n\r\n- `extensions/ollama/src/stream.ts`: Hoist `reader = response.body.getReader()` so the `finally` block can call `reader?.releaseLock()` (defensive `try/catch`) before `await release()`. The reader no longer relies on implicit fetch cleanup.\r\n- `src/infra/http-error-body.ts`:\r\n  - Pre??heck `Content-Length` on body??ess responses and short??ircuit oversize declared bodies with an empty (truncated) result, never materializing the body.\r\n  - Wrap body??ess responses in a cancelable `ReadableStream` and route them through the shared `readResponseTextPrefix` helper. The shared helper cancels the source once it has seen `limits.maxBytes` bytes, and the wrapper honors that cancel by refusing to enqueue further chunks. The old `arrayBuffer()` + `subarray` path is removed because the slice never reduced the allocated buffer.\r\n- `extensions/firecrawl/src/firecrawl-client.ts`: After the JSON-payload clone is consumed, call `await response.body?.cancel().catch(() => undefined)` to release the underlying body.\r\n\r\n## User Impact\r\n\r\nGateway and plugin processes no longer leak stream resources or risk large memory allocations on error responses. Connection pools recover faster after Ollama/Firecrawl failures, and the `http-error-body` shared fallback no longer allocates the complete body before applying `limits.maxBytes`.\r\n\r\n## Evidence\r\n\r\n### Acceptance criteria\r\n\r\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/infra/http-error-body.test.ts` ??every test passes, including the new body-less safety cases (`rejects an oversize Content-Length without materializing the body`, `streams a body-less response and stops at limits.maxBytes (cancelled wrapper)`, `accepts a body-less response whose declared Content-Length fits within limits.maxBytes`).\r\n- `pnpm test extensions/firecrawl/src/firecrawl-client.test.ts` ??passes; the new `response.body?.cancel()` call is exercised by the existing `payload-success` and `payload-fail` cases.\r\n- `pnpm test extensions/ollama` ??passes; the success path is unchanged and the cleanup `finally` block exercises `releaseLock()` on both success and error termination.\r\n\r\n### Real behavior proof (redacted)\r\n\r\n- **Pre-check short-circuit**: a synthetic `Response` with `body: null`, `headers: { \"content-length\": \"10000\" }`, and a `text()` that increments a counter is fed into `readResponseBodySnippet` with `maxBytes: 64`. The counter is `0` after the call and the returned string is `\"\"`. The body is never materialized.\r\n- **Bounded streaming read (body-less)**: a 200??yte body-less response is fed in with `maxBytes: 50`. The result has UTF?? byte length `??50` and is strictly shorter than the source. The internal `pull()` enqueues a 16 KiB chunk and stops enqueueing as soon as the shared `readResponseTextPrefix` reader cancels the source.\r\n- **Ollama reader release**: a spy `getReader` is mounted and the stream is interrupted with a non??K response. `reader.releaseLock()` is observed in the test harness before `release()` returns.\r\n- **Firecrawl cancel**: a `Response` whose `body.cancel()` resolves is passed through the JSON??rror branch; `cancel()` is observed and the success-path `parse` is reached when the JSON payload is valid.\r\n\r\n### Lint and format\r\n\r\n- `pnpm exec oxfmt --check` passes for the three changed files.\r\n- `pnpm exec oxlint` passes for the three changed files (no new warnings introduced).\r\n\r\n### Note on `process/child-process` shard\r\n\r\nThe `checks-node-core-test-nondist-shard` failure visible on the latest CI run is a pre-existing `process/child-process.test.ts > drains active descendant output after the parent exits` flake that is already red on `main` (tracked by the maintainer team). It is unrelated to this PR's changed files and, per `CONTRIBUTING.md`, is not fixed by contributor PRs.",
